Zigzag level-order traversal (alternating left-right and right-left):

function zigzag(root):
    if root is null: return []
    result = []
    q = Deque()
    q.pushBack(root)
    leftToRight = True
    while q not empty:
        size = q.size(); level = []
        for i in 0..size-1:
            if leftToRight:
                node = q.popFront()
                if node.left:  q.pushBack(node.left)
                if node.right: q.pushBack(node.right)
            else:
                node = q.popBack()
                if node.right: q.pushFront(node.right)
                if node.left:  q.pushFront(node.left)
            level.append(node.val)
        result.append(level)
        leftToRight = not leftToRight
    return result

Time complexity?

Recursive DFS on a graph (adjacency list, V vertices, E edges):

function dfs(graph, start, visited):
    visited.add(start)
    process(start)
    for neighbor in graph[start]:
        if neighbor not in visited:
            dfs(graph, neighbor, visited)

Time and space complexity?

Iterative DFS using an explicit stack:

function iterativeDFS(graph, start):
    stack = [start]
    visited = {}
    while stack not empty:
        v = stack.pop()
        if v in visited: continue
        visited.add(v)
        process(v)
        for neighbor in graph[v]:
            if neighbor not in visited:
                stack.push(neighbor)

Compared to recursive DFS, what is different about the traversal order?

Detecting a cycle in an undirected graph using DFS:

function hasCycle(graph, v, visited, parent):
    visited.add(v)
    for neighbor in graph[v]:
        if neighbor not in visited:
            if hasCycle(graph, neighbor, visited, v):
                return True
        elif neighbor != parent:
            return True
    return False

Time complexity (V vertices, E edges)?

Topological sort using DFS (Kahn's variant uses BFS; this is DFS-based):

function topoSort(graph, v, visited, result):
    visited.add(v)
    for neighbor in graph[v]:
        if neighbor not in visited:
            topoSort(graph, neighbor, visited, result)
    result.prepend(v)   // or push to stack, reverse at end

Time and space complexity for a DAG with V vertices and E edges?

DFS on a directed graph to detect a back edge (cycle in directed graph):

function dfs(graph, v, visited, recStack):
    visited.add(v)
    recStack.add(v)
    for neighbor in graph[v]:
        if neighbor not in visited:
            if dfs(graph, neighbor, visited, recStack):
                return True
        elif neighbor in recStack:
            return True
    recStack.remove(v)
    return False

Why is recStack needed instead of just visited?

